1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,348
A book on a tilted desk stays
put, until one tilt too many.

2
00:00:04,348 --> 00:00:07,299
A sand pile never gets
steeper than one slope.

3
00:00:07,699 --> 00:00:10,222
Something holds them,
but only up to a limit.

4
00:00:10,672 --> 00:00:14,684
Aarav props up his drawing board,
with his geometry box on it.

5
00:00:15,034 --> 00:00:16,436
He tilts it slowly.

6
00:00:16,436 --> 00:00:18,879
Still, still, then it shoots down.

7
00:00:18,879 --> 00:00:21,985
Two boxes taped together
slip at the same angle.

8
00:00:21,985 --> 00:00:22,885
Why?

9
00:00:22,981 --> 00:00:25,149
The answer is limiting friction.

10
00:00:25,749 --> 00:00:27,582
So what is limiting friction?

11
00:00:27,932 --> 00:00:32,252
Static friction pushes back just as
hard as you push, but only up to a

12
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:33,152
limit.

13
00:00:32,832 --> 00:00:36,763
That largest value, just before
slipping, is limiting friction.

14
00:00:37,163 --> 00:00:42,041
Limiting friction equals mu s times
N: the coefficient of static friction,

15
00:00:42,041 --> 00:00:43,754
times the normal force.

16
00:00:44,404 --> 00:00:45,975
To the motion notebook.

17
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:49,587
A two kilogram block on a
board, mu s zero point five.

18
00:00:49,937 --> 00:00:51,031
Tilt the board.

19
00:00:51,031 --> 00:00:55,670
The pull down the slope, m g sine theta,
grows, and friction grows to match

20
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:56,570
it.

21
00:00:56,496 --> 00:00:59,923
At ten degrees, both are
three point four newtons.

22
00:00:59,923 --> 00:01:01,944
At twenty, six point seven.

23
00:01:02,344 --> 00:01:06,908
But the limit, mu s N, shrinks, as
the block presses less into the board.

24
00:01:07,308 --> 00:01:09,936
At twenty six point
six degrees, they meet.

25
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:13,013
Friction is at its limit,
eight point eight newtons.

26
00:01:13,413 --> 00:01:15,272
A hair more, and it slips.

27
00:01:15,272 --> 00:01:18,388
Kinetic friction, about
seven newtons, takes over.

28
00:01:18,388 --> 00:01:19,525
The graph drops.

29
00:01:20,125 --> 00:01:21,182
Slow-mo replay.

30
00:01:21,182 --> 00:01:23,415
Freeze at the moment of slipping.

31
00:01:23,765 --> 00:01:26,170
Down the slope, m g sine theta.

32
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:28,833
Up the slope, friction at its limit.

33
00:01:28,833 --> 00:01:30,761
N balances m g cos theta.

34
00:01:30,761 --> 00:01:32,305
They are just equal.

35
00:01:32,955 --> 00:01:35,083
Now the rule, one step at a time.

36
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:36,333
Step one.

37
00:01:36,277 --> 00:01:39,817
At rest, friction balances
the pull down the slope.

38
00:01:39,817 --> 00:01:41,680
f equals m g sine theta.

39
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:43,339
Step two.

40
00:01:43,339 --> 00:01:46,488
Into the slope, N equals m g cos theta.

41
00:01:47,238 --> 00:01:48,151
Step three.

42
00:01:48,151 --> 00:01:50,085
Static friction has a limit.

43
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:52,098
f is at most mu s times N.

44
00:01:52,848 --> 00:01:53,925
Step four.

45
00:01:53,925 --> 00:01:59,259
About to slip: m g sine theta
r equals mu s m g cos theta r.

46
00:02:00,009 --> 00:02:01,078
Step five.

47
00:02:01,078 --> 00:02:04,383
Cancel m g: tan theta r equals mu s.

48
00:02:04,383 --> 00:02:07,111
Theta r is the angle of repose.

49
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:08,996
Remember this.

50
00:02:08,996 --> 00:02:12,446
At the angle of repose,
tan theta equals mu s.

51
00:02:12,446 --> 00:02:14,080
The mass cancels out.

52
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:16,366
Now, Aarav's puzzle.

53
00:02:16,366 --> 00:02:17,523
Link by link.

54
00:02:17,873 --> 00:02:20,403
A heavier box pulls harder down the slope.

55
00:02:20,803 --> 00:02:25,226
But it also presses harder, so
N and the limit mu s N grow too.

56
00:02:25,626 --> 00:02:28,497
Both grow with m, in the same ratio.

57
00:02:28,497 --> 00:02:29,676
So m cancels.

58
00:02:30,326 --> 00:02:31,489
Side by side.

59
00:02:31,489 --> 00:02:36,970
At fifteen degrees, friction is five
point one newtons, just m g sine theta.

60
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:39,188
The limit is nine point five.

61
00:02:39,588 --> 00:02:42,814
At twenty six point six,
friction reaches the limit.

62
00:02:42,814 --> 00:02:45,373
Static friction gives only what is needed.

63
00:02:45,973 --> 00:02:46,884
Your turn.

64
00:02:46,884 --> 00:02:51,886
At fifteen degrees, is the friction
on the block nine point five newtons?

65
00:02:51,886 --> 00:02:53,225
Pause and decide.

66
00:02:56,425 --> 00:02:57,325
No!

67
00:02:56,901 --> 00:02:58,443
Only five point one.

68
00:02:58,443 --> 00:03:01,868
It reaches mu s N only
at the point of slipping.

69
00:03:02,518 --> 00:03:03,698
One more pair.

70
00:03:03,698 --> 00:03:08,116
The angle of repose is the steepest
slope where a body can rest.

71
00:03:08,116 --> 00:03:10,006
Tan theta r equals mu s.

72
00:03:10,406 --> 00:03:14,989
The angle of friction, lambda, is
between N and the total contact force

73
00:03:14,989 --> 00:03:16,070
at the limit.

74
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:19,601
Tan lambda equals f L
over N, so it is mu s too.

75
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:22,155
So theta r equals lambda.

76
00:03:22,755 --> 00:03:23,839
Now you try.

77
00:03:23,839 --> 00:03:26,995
Dry sand has mu s of
zero point seven five.

78
00:03:26,995 --> 00:03:30,036
What is the steepest slope of a sand pile?

79
00:03:30,036 --> 00:03:31,383
Pause and decide.

80
00:03:34,583 --> 00:03:36,536
About thirty seven degrees!

81
00:03:36,536 --> 00:03:39,921
Tan thirty seven is about
zero point seven five.

82
00:03:40,571 --> 00:03:42,307
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:42,657 --> 00:03:46,640
Aarav presses a one point two
kilogram book against a wall.

84
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,508
Mu s is zero point four.

85
00:03:48,508 --> 00:03:51,542
What is the smallest
push that holds it up?

86
00:03:52,042 --> 00:03:55,254
The weight is one point
two times nine point eight.

87
00:03:55,254 --> 00:03:57,239
Eleven point seven six newtons.

88
00:03:57,989 --> 00:04:01,217
Friction must hold that up,
and here N is the push F.

89
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:05,861
At the limit, mu s F equals
eleven point seven six.

90
00:04:05,861 --> 00:04:09,700
So F is eleven point seven
six over zero point four.

91
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:12,403
Twenty nine point four newtons.

92
00:04:12,403 --> 00:04:14,458
Any less, and the book slides.

93
00:04:15,108 --> 00:04:16,571
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:16,921 --> 00:04:20,905
Many students say slipping starts
when tan theta equals mu k.

95
00:04:21,305 --> 00:04:25,165
But a resting body slips when
static friction runs out.

96
00:04:25,165 --> 00:04:27,330
So tan theta r equals mu s.

97
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:29,764
Mu k matters only once it moves.

98
00:04:30,414 --> 00:04:31,812
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:31,812 --> 00:04:34,993
Bodies about to slip, on
slopes and against walls.

100
00:04:35,593 --> 00:04:36,873
Exam level now.

101
00:04:36,873 --> 00:04:42,025
A five kilogram block is on a thirty
seven degree incline, mu s zero point

102
00:04:42,025 --> 00:04:42,925
five.

103
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:46,158
g is ten, sine thirty
seven is zero point six.

104
00:04:46,158 --> 00:04:50,399
What least force along the
incline just starts it moving up?

105
00:04:50,399 --> 00:04:51,734
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:57,134 --> 00:04:58,559
The answer is C.

107
00:04:58,559 --> 00:04:59,742
Fifty newtons.

108
00:05:00,242 --> 00:05:03,947
Down the slope, m g sine
theta is thirty newtons.

109
00:05:03,947 --> 00:05:05,015
N is forty.

110
00:05:05,515 --> 00:05:09,497
Moving up, limiting friction acts
down the slope: twenty newtons.

111
00:05:09,997 --> 00:05:12,513
So F is thirty plus twenty, fifty.

112
00:05:12,513 --> 00:05:15,181
Ten newtons only stops it sliding down.

113
00:05:15,781 --> 00:05:17,243
Here is your recap.

114
00:05:17,243 --> 00:05:18,746
Screenshot this card.

115
00:05:21,946 --> 00:05:23,877
Can you answer these without help?

116
00:05:24,277 --> 00:05:25,177
One.

117
00:05:24,779 --> 00:05:29,415
Why do a light and a heavy box of
one material slip at the same angle?

118
00:05:30,315 --> 00:05:31,215
Two.

119
00:05:30,886 --> 00:05:34,118
Is static friction always equal to mu s N?

120
00:05:34,118 --> 00:05:35,214
When is it?

121
00:05:36,114 --> 00:05:37,014
Three.

122
00:05:36,698 --> 00:05:41,390
What is the angle of friction, and
why does it equal the angle of repose?

123
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:46,809
Next time: why is a heavy box so
much easier to move on wheels?

124
00:05:46,809 --> 00:05:48,023
Rolling friction.
